Z||000000||wwh0001||12-24-1999||12:46 PM||||I got out my timing light to adjust my timing on the 87 gt (V6) and the timing marks are barly visable. What I need it know is what degrees are the marks ? This is a typical timing gauge<BR>-5    0    5    10   15   20   <BR>- |    ||     |      |    | <BR>I can see the marks on the GT but can't tell what the degree is suppose to be.<P>Any Help ? WHat should this gauge be for a GT.  Thanks<P><BR>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 12-24-1999).]<P>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 12-24-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 12-24-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||12-24-1999||01:55 PM||||Facing the engine  from the passenger side you will see a series of points and vallys on the edge or the timing plate. the last point to the left is 12 degrees the first depression to the right of it, is 10 Degrees. the next point to the right is 8 Degrees and so on. 2 degres between peak and vally so to speak.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000002||wwh0001||12-24-1999||02:17 PM||||Thanks. the timing is suppose to be 10 based on the deck lid sticker.  Do you know if there is a more opitmal setting? Would advancing the timing do any good?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||12-24-1999||02:37 PM||||I have an 87 GT also. My deck lid says 10 Degrees and thats what I use. You put it in diagnostic mode with the paperclip to set timing of course.<BR> Guess you could play with a little deviation. maybe record the amount of change and test drive it. I wouldn't think you would have a lot of latitude with the adjustment.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||12-25-1999||04:32 AM||||I do not remember exactly what the sticker says, but isn't the 10 degreese a starting point. Yhen you average the final setting between the #1 And #4 readings. To obtain optimal preformance?<BR>Good Luck.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
